{
  "id": "d0dabc92-5387-4f8e-ab81-c20a1156ffe9",
  "productId": "openmrs",
  "title": "Lab Order Fulfiller Status Save Flow",
  "updatedAt": 1793548755882,
  "answers": [
    {
      "id": "87oegSaiRd5G1Jyo",
      "productId": "openmrs",
      "question": "When a user saves the fulfiller status and comment of a lab order in the patient chart, what code runs from the frontend request down to the database write? List the frontend function that sends the request, the server-side REST handler, the core service implementation, and the data-access method.",
      "generation": "g-b59a21",
      "kind": "grounded",
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "Saving the fulfiller status and comment of a lab order in the patient chart crosses two repositories: the React frontend in **openmrs-esm-patient-chart** and the OpenMRS backend split across **openmrs-module-webservices.rest** (the REST layer) and **openmrs-core** (service and database access). The chain has four named steps you asked about.",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "## 1. Frontend function that sends the request",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "The lab results form component `ExportedLabResultsForm` is what the user sees. When the user clicks Save, its submit handler `saveLabResults` runs [1]. This handler builds two payloads:",
          "citations": [
            1
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "- `orderDiscontinuationPayload`, which stops the existing order [2]\n- `resultsStatusPayload`, which is exactly the fulfiller status and comment: `{ fulfillerStatus: 'COMPLETED', fulfillerComment: 'Test Results Entered' }` [3]",
          "citations": [
            2,
            3
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Depending on whether results already exist, the handler calls either `updateOrderResult` (fresh entry) [4] or `completeOrderWithSavedResults` (finishing an earlier partial attempt) [5]. Both end in the same helper, which is the function that actually pushes the fulfiller status and comment to the server:",
          "citations": [
            4,
            5
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "- `updateOrderFulfillerDetails` issues `POST {restBaseUrl}/order/{orderUuid}/fulfillerdetails/` with the payload as the JSON body [6]",
          "citations": [
            6
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "So on the frontend, the function sending the request is `updateOrderFulfillerDetails` [6].",
          "citations": [
            6
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "## 2. Server-side REST handler",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "The URL `/order/{uuid}/fulfillerdetails/` maps to a **sub-resource** of the Order resource, defined by the `@SubResource` annotation on `FulfillerDetailsResource2_3` [7]. On a POST, the REST framework calls its `save` method, which forwards the three fields to the core service [8].",
          "citations": [
            7,
            8
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "The request body is bound into a small delegate object, `FulfillerDetails2_3`, which extends `FulfillerDetails2_2` and adds an accession number alongside `fulfillerStatus` and `fulfillerComment` [9]. The older `FulfillerDetails2_2` holds the two fields you asked about [10].",
          "citations": [
            9,
            10
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "## 3. Core service implementation",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "The handler calls `OrderService.updateOrderFulfillerStatus(...)`, defined on the service interface [11] and implemented in `OrderServiceImpl`. The 4-argument overload copies the status, comment and accession number onto the `Order` object (each guarded by a null check) and then calls `saveOrderInternal` [12].",
          "citations": [
            11,
            12
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "## 4. Data-access method",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "`saveOrderInternal` handles the order-number and auto-expire bookkeeping, then hands off to the DAO [13]. The DAO interface declares `saveOrder` [14], and the Hibernate implementation is where the write reaches the database: it delegates to `HibernateUtil.saveOrUpdate` on the current Hibernate session, which issues the INSERT/UPDATE for the order row [15].",
          "citations": [
            13,
            14,
            15
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "## Full flow at a glance",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "```mermaid\n---\ntitle: Lab order fulfiller save path\n---\nflowchart TD\n  UI[\"Lab results form confirm\"] --> Save[\"saveLabResults builds payloads\"]\n  Save --> Helper[\"updateOrderFulfillerDetails POSTs\"]\n  Helper --> Rest[\"FulfillerDetailsResource2_3 save\"]\n  Rest --> Svc[\"OrderServiceImpl.updateOrderFulfillerStatus\"]\n  Svc --> Internal[\"saveOrderInternal\"]\n  Internal --> Dao[\"HibernateOrderDAO.saveOrder\"]\n  Dao --> Db[\"Hibernate saveOrUpdate writes order row\"]\n```",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Worth noting: the fulfiller status update is a **second** request. The same handler first saves the observations and discontinues the order in one `POST /encounter/{uuid}` call, so the backend commits or rolls back those together, and only then posts the fulfiller details [16].",
          "citations": [
            16
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "## What I could not verify",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "- I read the patient-chart frontend, the webservices.rest sub-resource and the openmrs-core service/DAO from their default branches. I did not confirm which tagged releases contain these exact versions.\n- Hibernate's `saveOrUpdate` mapping of the `Order` entity to its table and columns (the SQL itself) lives in mapping files I did not open; the statement above ends at the Hibernate session call.",
          "citations": []
        }
      ],
